C = [
("h1", "10. Offering and Sacrifice"),
("p", "Claim: Paul also uses the language of the altar and the feast. Christ is “our passover,” he is “an offering and a sacrifice to God,” he is God’s own Son who is delivered up “for us all,” and he is sent “for sin.” The language is a handful of figures, each used for a particular purpose in its letter. The passages do not run together into a doctrine of sacrifice, and the volume does not make one."),
("sub", "10.1 “Christ our passover” (1 Corinthians 5:7)"),
("p", "“Purge out therefore the old leaven, that ye may be a new lump, as ye are unleavened. For even Christ our passover is sacrificed for us” (1 Corinthians 5:7). The sentence is a reason for a command. Paul is writing about a man in the church at Corinth whom the church had not put away, and he gives the command to purge out the leaven. The reason is that Christ our passover is sacrificed for us. The figure is drawn from the feast, and the leaven belongs to it. The verse does not say more of the passover than that it was sacrificed “for us,” and it does not set out a doctrine of the Passover. The volume reads the figure as Paul uses it, as the ground of a call to purity."),
("sub", "10.2 “An offering and a sacrifice to God” (Ephesians 5:2)"),
("p", "“And walk in love, as Christ also hath loved us, and hath given himself for us an offering and a sacrifice to God for a sweetsmelling savour” (Ephesians 5:2). Here again the Cross is an example as well as a ground. Christ loved us, and gave himself for us. The offering and sacrifice are “to God,” and the result is described as “a sweetsmelling savour.” The command is to walk in love. The passage does not explain the offering, and it does not say what the savour is for. It says that Christ’s giving of himself was an offering and a sacrifice to God, and it draws from it an exhortation."),
("sub", "10.3 “God sending his own Son… and for sin” (Romans 8:3)"),
("p", "“For what the law could not do, in that it was weak through the flesh, God sending his own Son in the likeness of sinful flesh, and for sin, condemned sin in the flesh” (Romans 8:3). The verse has God as its subject: God sending his own Son, God condemning sin in the flesh. The phrase “and for sin” may be read with the sending (as an offering for sin) or as a separate clause, and the volume leaves the reading to the verse. What the verse says is that the law could not do what God did, that the law was weak through the flesh, and that God condemned sin in the flesh. It is the nearest statement in Romans to a judgment executed on sin itself."),
("p", "“He that spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how shall he not with him also freely give us all things?” (Romans 8:32). The verb is “delivered up,” the same family as Romans 4:25. The sentence is an argument from the greater to the lesser. If God gave the Son, he will give the lesser things. The “us all” is the “us” of the chapter, those who are in Christ Jesus (Romans 8:1), and the volume reads it so."),
("sub", "10.4 “Gave himself” (Galatians 1:4; Titus 2:14; 1 Timothy 2:6)"),
("p", "Paul uses the verb “gave himself” in several places. “Who gave himself for our sins, that he might deliver us from this present evil world” (Galatians 1:4). “Who gave himself for us, that he might redeem us from all iniquity, and purify unto himself a peculiar people, zealous of good works” (Titus 2:14). “Who gave himself a ransom for all, to be testified in due time” (1 Timothy 2:6). The giving is voluntary in each. The objects differ: our sins, us, all. The purposes differ: to deliver us from this present evil world, to redeem us and purify a people, a ransom to be testified in due time. The volume does not merge them."),
("sub", "10.5 “Obedient unto death” and “the enemies of the cross” (Philippians 2:8; 3:18)"),
("p", "“And being found in fashion as a man, he humbled himself, and became obedient unto death, even the death of the cross” (Philippians 2:8). The Cross here is the lowest point in a descent, and the passage is written to call the readers to a mind. It is not an account of what the death accomplished. Later in the same letter Paul writes of “the enemies of the cross of Christ” (Philippians 3:18), and says that he tells it weeping. The Cross is in this letter a mark of humility and of loyalty, and the volume notes the uses without making them an account of atonement."),
("ctbl", "Offering, sacrifice and gift in the passages of this chapter.", [
    ("Passage", "The figure", "What it is used for"),
    ("1 Corinthians 5:7", "Passover sacrificed", "The ground of a call to purge out the old leaven"),
    ("Ephesians 5:2", "Offering and sacrifice to God", "The pattern for a walk in love"),
    ("Romans 8:3", "God sending his Son, and for sin", "What the law could not do; sin condemned in the flesh"),
    ("Romans 8:32", "Delivered up for us all", "The ground of confidence in God’s giving"),
    ("Galatians 1:4", "Gave himself for our sins", "Deliverance from this present evil world"),
    ("Titus 2:14", "Gave himself for us", "Redemption from iniquity and a purified people"),
    ("1 Timothy 2:6", "Gave himself a ransom for all", "To be testified in due time"),
    ("Philippians 2:8", "Obedient unto death", "The mind to be in the readers"),
  ], [0.26, 0.34, 0.40]),
("sub", "10.6 What the chapter does not do"),
("p", "The chapter does not argue that the language of the altar is the real account and the others figures of it. It does not argue the reverse. It does not turn the passover of 1 Corinthians 5:7 into the Passover of Exodus 12, which is the subject of other volumes. It does not take up Hebrews or the first letter of Peter, whose language of sacrifice and blood is addressed to other readers in other settings, and which the study on redemption carries for its own subject. Paul’s letters are the volume’s ground, and in them the language of sacrifice is one language among several."),
]
